# Line endings: every text file is stored AND checked out with LF.
#
# `eol=lf` is the half that makes this hold on Windows. It overrides the local
# core.autocrlf setting, so a clone on Windows gets LF in the working tree, not
# CRLF. Without it git checks out whatever the machine happens to prefer, and
# the same file differs between two people's disks while looking identical in
# every editor.
#
# `text=auto` still lets git decide text-vs-binary by inspecting content. That
# guess is reliable but it is a guess, so every binary type is declared
# explicitly below and can never be normalised whatever the sniffing decides.
#
# This is not housekeeping. Two recorded failures in these orgs:
#   - A carriage return on the first line of a shell script piped to bash on
#     Ubuntu fails as "set: pipefail: invalid option name", and stops every
#     heredoc terminator matching its opener - which reads as a broken script
#     rather than a broken file format.
#   - A Windows tool rewrote page headers and silently converted committed pages
#     from LF to CRLF. Nothing errored, nothing rendered differently, and the
#     only visible sign was a whole-file diff on a one-line edit.
*                text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files are LF too, deliberately - do not "fix" this to eol=crlf.
# Maven's and Gradle's generated .gitattributes force CRLF for *.cmd / *.bat, so
# the exception looks obligatory. It was measured on Windows 11 (2026-09-08) and
# it is not: cmd.exe ran LF-only batch files identically to CRLF ones, including
# a backward `goto` loop across a 25 KB file, `call :label` with a return, and
# parenthesised if/else and for blocks with delayed expansion.

